<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euthydemus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 225–200 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euthydemus I was a Greco-Bactrian king from Magnesia who overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-ii">Diodotus II</a> around 225 BCE and founded the Euthydemid dynasty. He withstood a two-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> by the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, who in about 206 BCE recognised him as king and offered a daughter in marriage to his son Demetrius I. Under his successors the Greco-Bactrians expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i.jpg" alt="Euthydemus I" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group;[1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Euthydemos_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
